WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
The first wave idea is one of the most important in the whole module.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.960
When a wave crosses a rope, a spring, water, or a stadium crowd pattern, the thing that travels is the disturbance pattern.

3
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.440
The material itself usually does not march from one side of the system to the other.

4
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.920
Each point in the medium responds locally.

5
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.400
It rises and falls, sways, compresses and relaxes, or pulses around its own place.

6
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.880
That is why good answers keep two stories separate.

7
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.360
Story one is the front or pattern traveling across the system.

8
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:43.840
Story two is each local point in the medium only reacting where it already is.

9
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:49.320
Keep this takeaway in view: A wave is a traveling disturbance pattern carried by local motion in the medium.

10
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.800
Traveling story means the pattern moves.

11
00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.280
The front crosses the rope, spring, or water surface from one region to another.

12
00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.760
Local story means the medium responds.

13
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.240
Each point only oscillates, pulses, or compresses around its own location.

14
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:16.667
This diagram is useful because it puts the two stories on one board without mixing them.

15
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:22.094
The highlighted front or same-phase pattern is shown crossing the field.

16
00:01:22.094 --> 00:01:27.521
That is the part that travels.

17
00:01:27.521 --> 00:01:32.948
But the individual pads in the field do not all slide across with it.

18
00:01:32.948 --> 00:01:38.375
Each pad only moves around its own place when the front reaches it.

19
00:01:38.375 --> 00:01:43.802
A strong answer reads the picture in that order.

20
00:01:43.802 --> 00:01:49.228
First identify the traveling feature.

21
00:01:49.228 --> 00:01:54.655
Then identify the local response of the medium.

22
00:01:54.655 --> 00:02:00.082
Finally keep the speed calculation attached to the front travel, not to one local pad.

23
00:02:00.082 --> 00:02:05.509
That order turns a vague wave description into a precise physical explanation.

24
00:02:05.509 --> 00:02:10.936
Keep this takeaway in view: Watch one point and the whole front separately before describing the wave.

25
00:02:10.936 --> 00:02:16.363
Front: Use the moving front to talk about wave travel across the system.

26
00:02:16.363 --> 00:02:21.790
Point in medium: Use one chosen pad to describe what local motion actually looks like.

27
00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:27.706
Imagine a stadium wave traveling around an arena.

28
00:02:27.706 --> 00:02:33.622
The bright arch of raised arms clearly moves around the stands, yet no single fan has to run around the stadium to make that happen.

29
00:02:33.622 --> 00:02:39.538
Each person only stands up and sits down near one seat.

30
00:02:39.538 --> 00:02:45.453
The pattern moves because the response passes from neighbor to neighbor.

31
00:02:45.453 --> 00:02:51.369
That is exactly why the analogy works for many wave questions.

32
00:02:51.369 --> 00:02:57.285
It keeps local action and traveling pattern apart.

33
00:02:57.285 --> 00:03:03.201
It also explains why you can measure a wave speed from how fast the visible front crosses the arena, even though no fan is personally running at that speed around the whole stadium.

34
00:03:03.201 --> 00:03:09.117
Keep this takeaway in view: The pattern can travel far while every local part of the medium stays local.

35
00:03:09.117 --> 00:03:15.032
One seat means local response.

36
00:03:15.032 --> 00:03:20.948
One fan only rises and lowers near one position.

37
00:03:20.948 --> 00:03:26.864
Whole stadium means pattern travel.

38
00:03:26.864 --> 00:03:32.780
The visible arch of motion crosses the arena from place to place.

39
00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.936
Once the physical picture is secure, the basic numerical rule becomes simple.

40
00:03:37.936 --> 00:03:43.091
Wave speed equals the distance traveled by the front divided by the time taken.

41
00:03:43.091 --> 00:03:48.247
That wording matters.

42
00:03:48.247 --> 00:03:53.403
The distance must be the distance crossed by the wavefront or disturbance pattern.

43
00:03:53.403 --> 00:03:58.559
The time must be the travel time for that front.

44
00:03:58.559 --> 00:04:03.714
This is not a story about one particle moving across the whole system.

45
00:04:03.714 --> 00:04:08.870
If a front crosses twelve metres in three seconds, the wave speed is four metres per second.

46
00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:14.026
That tells you how quickly the disturbance travels through the medium.

47
00:04:14.026 --> 00:04:19.181
Keep this takeaway in view: Wave speed is a front-travel quantity, not a one-particle journey quantity.

48
00:04:19.181 --> 00:04:24.337
Distance: Measure how far the front travels across the medium.

49
00:04:24.337 --> 00:04:29.493
Time: Measure the time for that front travel.

50
00:04:29.493 --> 00:04:34.649
wave speed = distance traveled by the front / time.

51
00:04:34.649 --> 00:04:39.804
use the front distance, not the path of one particle.

52
00:04:39.804 --> 00:04:44.960
Keep the local particle motion and the front travel story in separate slots before you substitute numbers.

53
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:51.436
Another useful pattern is the idea of same phase.

54
00:04:51.436 --> 00:04:57.913
One front can join all the points that are at the same stage of the oscillation together.

55
00:04:57.913 --> 00:05:04.389
As time passes, that same-phase front appears in a new place.

56
00:05:04.389 --> 00:05:10.865
This is why waves can carry information and energy across space even though the medium often only oscillates locally.

57
00:05:10.865 --> 00:05:17.342
The front keeps advancing, the medium keeps responding locally, and the repeating cycle passes from region to region.

58
00:05:17.342 --> 00:05:23.818
Once you see that pattern, many later wave ideas become much easier because you stop asking the wrong question about whether the medium must travel across the whole distance.

59
00:05:23.818 --> 00:05:30.295
Keep this takeaway in view: A wavefront travels because matching-phase motion appears in new places over time.

60
00:05:30.295 --> 00:05:36.771
Same phase means one front.

61
00:05:36.771 --> 00:05:43.247
Join matching points in the cycle to visualize a wavefront.

62
00:05:43.247 --> 00:05:49.724
New place later means front travel.

63
00:05:49.724 --> 00:05:56.200
The same pattern reaches a new region after more time passes.

64
00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.982
Try three checks.

65
00:05:59.982 --> 00:06:03.765
First, what actually travels from one side of the system to the other?

66
00:06:03.765 --> 00:06:07.547
The disturbance pattern or wavefront.

67
00:06:07.547 --> 00:06:11.329
Second, what does one point in the medium usually do?

68
00:06:11.329 --> 00:06:15.112
It oscillates locally.

69
00:06:15.112 --> 00:06:18.894
Third, when you calculate wave speed, what distance do you use?

70
00:06:18.894 --> 00:06:22.676
The distance traveled by the front.

71
00:06:22.676 --> 00:06:26.458
These checks matter because they stop a very common wave misconception before it reaches the example.

72
00:06:26.458 --> 00:06:30.241
Keep this takeaway in view: The traveling pattern and the local medium motion are now cleanly separated.

73
00:06:30.241 --> 00:06:34.023
Traveling feature means the disturbance.

74
00:06:34.023 --> 00:06:37.805
That is the part that crosses the system.

75
00:06:37.805 --> 00:06:41.588
Local feature means the medium point.

76
00:06:41.588 --> 00:06:45.370
That point only oscillates around its own place.

77
00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:50.787
You are ready for the worked example now.

78
00:06:50.787 --> 00:06:56.204
Start by naming the traveling feature of the wave.

79
00:06:56.204 --> 00:07:01.621
Then identify what one point in the medium is doing locally.

80
00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:07.038
If the example asks for wave speed, use the distance crossed by the front and the time taken.

81
00:07:07.038 --> 00:07:12.455
If the example asks what the particles are doing, describe local oscillation instead of whole-system travel.

82
00:07:12.455 --> 00:07:17.872
That sequence is the whole lesson method.

83
00:07:17.872 --> 00:07:23.288
Separate the pattern, the local response, and the speed calculation, and the example will make sense very quickly.

84
00:07:23.288 --> 00:07:28.705
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: pattern travel and local medium motion now have different roles.

85
00:07:28.705 --> 00:07:34.122
Step 1: Name the disturbance pattern that moves across the system.

86
00:07:34.122 --> 00:07:39.539
Step 2: Describe the local oscillation, pulse, or compression of the medium.

87
00:07:39.539 --> 00:07:44.956
identify the traveling front or disturbance.

88
00:07:44.956 --> 00:07:50.373
describe the local motion of one point in the medium.

89
00:07:50.373 --> 00:07:55.790
Strong wave answers begin by separating what travels from what merely oscillates locally.
